From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Richard Spindler <richard@lateralblast.com.au>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 46/55] scp: stop if the resume match status cannot be read

After the sink in "scp -Z" mode sends its resume reply it reads one byte, the
sender's match status. The return value of that read was ignored and match was
never initialised, so if the connection ended there the sink carried on with
whatever was in the variable (the previous file's value, or whatever the stack
held) and created the temporary file for the tail before failing on the next
read.

Initialise match and treat a failed read as a protocol error.
---
 scp.c | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/scp.c b/scp.c
index 43a7cd68e..f8befd4c1 100644
--- a/scp.c
+++ b/scp.c
@@ -1964,7 +1964,7 @@ sink(int argc, char **argv, const char *src)
 	char local_hashsum[HASH_LEN+1];
 	char tmpbuf[BUF_AND_HASH];
 	char outbuf[BUF_AND_HASH];
-	char match;
+	char match = 0;
 	int bad_match_flag = 0;
 	np = NULL; /* this was originally '/0' but that's wrong */
 	np_tmp = NULL;
@@ -2399,7 +2399,8 @@ sink(int argc, char **argv, const char *src)
 
 			/* the remote is always going to send a match status
 			 * so we need to read it so we don't get out of sync */
-			(void) atomicio(read, remin, &match, 1);
+			if (atomicio(read, remin, &match, 1) != 1)
+				SCREWUP("lost connection reading the resume match status");
 			if (match != 'M') {/*fragments do not match*/
 				/* expected response of F, M and NULL *but*
 				 * anything other than M is a failure
